"accusers"},{"greek":"ἐφρόντιζεν","english":"was concerned"},{"greek":"ἀπολογίαν","english":"defense"},{"greek":"ἀρκέσειν","english":"to suffice; future infinitive"},{"greek":"πεισθῶσιν","english":"are persuaded; aorist passive subjunctive"}]},{"greek":"Ὁ δὲ Σωκράτης ἀπεκρίνατο ὅτι ὁ βίος αὐτῷ πρὸς τὴν ἀπολογίαν παρεσκεύασται. οὐ γὰρ ἀδικῶν διέζη, ἀλλὰ τὰ δίκαια πράττων. τὰ δὲ ἔργα, ἃ οἱ φίλοι τεθέανται, οὐκ ὀλίγην μαρτυρίαν παρέχει. ἡ ἀπολογία οὐ λόγος ἐν μιᾷ ἡμέρᾳ κεκοσμημένος μόνον ἐστίν, ἀλλὰ καὶ βίος πολὺν χρόνον ἐξεταζόμενος. οὕτω τὴν προπαρασκευὴν ἑρμηνεύει.","gloss":[{"greek":"παρεσκεύασται","english":"has been prepared; perfect middle/passive"},{"greek":"διέζη","english":"lived through his life"},{"greek":"τεθέανται","english":"have observed; perfect of θεάομαι"},{"greek":"κεκοσμημένος","english":"adorned; perfect passive participle"},{"greek":"προπαρασκευὴν","english":"preparation"},{"greek":"ἑρμηνεύει","english":"interprets"}]},{"greek":"Ὁ Ἑρμογένης οὐκ εὐθὺς πέπεισται. ἐν γὰρ τοῖς δικαστηρίοις οἱ μὲν ἄδικοι ἀποφεύγουσιν, οἱ δὲ δίκαιοι κατακρίνονται. δεῖ οὖν καὶ πρὸς τὴν τῶν ἀκουόντων γνώμην λέγειν. ὁ δὲ Σωκράτης περὶ τοῦ δαιμονίου διηγεῖται. ὅτε πρὸς τὴν ἀπολογίαν ἐτράπετο, ἐναντίον αὐτῷ ἐγένετο τὸ σημεῖον. ταύτην τὴν ἀπόκρισιν ὁ Ἑρμογένης μετέδωκε τῷ Ξενοφῶντι.","gloss":[{"greek":"πέπεισται","english":"has been persuaded, is convinced"},{"greek":"ἀποφεύγουσιν","english":"are acquitted"},{"greek":"δαιμονίου","english":"divine sign"},{"greek":"ἐτράπετο","english":"turned"},{"greek":"σημεῖον","english":"sign"},{"greek":"μετέδωκε","english":"communicated; not a dated first-news claim"}]},{"greek":"Ὁ Σωκράτης καὶ τὸ γῆρας σκοπεῖ. φοβεῖται μὴ ὁ χρόνος τὴν μνήμην καὶ τὴν διάνοιαν βλάπτῃ. ὁ παρὼν βίος αὐτῷ ἱκανὸς δοκεῖ, καὶ τὸν θάνατον οὐ πάντων τῶν κακῶν μέγιστον νομίζει. οὐ τοῦτο διδάσκει ὁ Ξενοφῶν, ὅτι πᾶς ἄνθρωπος τὴν αὐτὴν κρίσιν ποιεῖται· τὴν τοῦ Σωκράτους γνώμην ἐξηγεῖται, καθ᾽ ἣν ἐκεῖνος τῇ δίκῃ προσέρχεται.","gloss":[{"greek":"γῆρας","english":"old age"},{"greek":"διάνοιαν","english":"understanding"},{"greek":"ἱκανὸς","english":"sufficient"},{"greek":"καθ᾽ ἣν","english":"in accordance with which"},{"greek":"προσέρχεται","english":"approaches"}]},{"greek":"Ἐν τῇ ἡμετέρᾳ ἀσκήσει διακρίνομεν τὸ γενόμενον καὶ τὸ νῦν ὑπάρχον. ὁ λόγος ἐγράφη· νῦν γέγραπται. ὁ φίλος ἐπείσθη· νῦν πέπεισται. πρὶν τὸν ἄλλον ἐλθεῖν, ἤδη ἐπέπειστο. τὰ μὲν πρῶτα ῥήματα πράξεις ὡς ὅλας δηλοῖ, τὰ δὲ ἄλλα τὴν ἐκ τῶν πράξεων κατάστασιν. μὴ πάντα τῷ αὐτῷ τρόπῳ μεταφράζωμεν.","gloss":[{"greek":"ὑπάρχον","english":"what holds"},{"greek":"γέγραπται","english":"stands written"},{"greek":"ἐπείσθη","english":"was persuaded"},{"greek":"ἐπέπειστο","english":"had been persuaded; pluperfect"},{"greek":"κατάστασιν","english":"state"},{"greek":"μεταφράζωμεν","english":"let us translate"}]},{"greek":"Τέλος ἐρωτῶμεν ποία ἀπολογία τῷ δικαστῇ πιστοτέρα φαίνεται. ὁ κεκοσμημένος λόγος δύναται ὠφελεῖν, ἀλλὰ δεῖ καὶ τὰ ἔργα ἐξετάζειν. ὁ δὲ ἐπαινούμενος βίος οὐκ ἀπαλλάττει τὸν ἀκροατὴν τοῦ κρίνειν. τὸν μὲν φίλον ἀκούομεν, τὸν δὲ συγγραφέα ἐξετάζομεν, καὶ τὸν λόγον πρὸς τὰ τεκμήρια συγκρίνομεν. οὕτως ἡ φιλία καὶ ἡ κρίσις ἅμα μένουσιν.","gloss":[{"greek":"ἀκροατὴν","english":"listener"},{"greek":"ἀπαλλάττει","english":"releases"},{"greek":"τεκμήρια","english":"evidence"},{"greek":"συγκρίνομεν","english":"we compare"},{"greek":"ἅμα","english":"together"}]}],"translation":"Xenophon writes about Socrates’ defense. He was not present in the court, but offers Hermogenes as a witness to the account. He asks why Socrates spoke so loftily. Others too wrote about him; Xenophon thinks that the man’s intention must be made clear, so that the lofty speech may not appear foolishness. The narrative therefore has a purpose.\n\nHermogenes was surprised to see Socrates discussing other matters. The trial was being prepared, and the accusers were not idle. The accusers’ names were being heard in the city, while the friend worried about Socrates’ safety. He therefore asked whether Socrates ought also to consider his defense. He thought that justice would not suffice if the jurors were not persuaded.\n\nSocrates replied that his life had been prepared by him as a defense. For he had lived not by doing wrong but by acting justly. The deeds that his friends have observed provide substantial testimony. A defense is not merely a speech adorned in a single day, but also a life examined over a long time. In this way he interprets preparation.\n\nHermogenes is not immediately convinced. In the courts the unjust are sometimes acquitted and the just condemned. One must therefore speak with the listeners’ judgment in mind. Socrates, however, tells of the divine sign. When he turned to preparing his defense, the sign opposed him. Hermogenes communicated this response to Xenophon.\n\nSocrates also considers old age. He fears that time may damage memory and understanding. His present life seems sufficient to him, and he does not regard death as the greatest of all evils. Xenophon does not teach here that every person makes the same judgment; he explains Socrates’ view, in accordance with which Socrates approaches the trial.\n\nIn our exercise we distinguish what happened from what now holds. The speech was written; now it stands written. The friend was persuaded; now he is convinced. Before the other person arrived, he had already been persuaded. The first verbs present actions as wholes, while the others present the state arising from the actions. Let us not translate everything in the same way.\n\nFinally we ask what kind of defense appears more credible to a judge. An adorned speech can help, but the deeds must also be examined. A praised life does not release the listener from judging. We listen to the friend, examine the writer, and compare the account with the evidence. In this way friendship and judgment remain together.\n","sourceCitation":"Xenophon, Apology 1–9. Independently composed course adaptation, not a verbatim quotation. https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1171/1171-h/1171-h.htm","notesMarkdown":"Adapted from Xenophon’s Apology 1–9. Xenophon identifies Hermogenes as his informant. Perfect and pluperfect descriptions and the concluding discussion are course compositions. This lesson concerns preparation, not the entire trial."}$new$::jsonb;
  lid uuid; rid uuid; current_reading jsonb; p jsonb; g jsonb; pi integer := 0; gi integer;
BEGIN
  SELECT id INTO STRICT lid FROM public.lessons WHERE slug='lesson-45' FOR UPDATE;
  SELECT content->'reading' INTO STRICT current_reading FROM public.lesson_content_overrides WHERE lesson_id=lid FOR UPDATE;
  IF current_reading = new_reading THEN RETURN; END IF;
  IF current_reading IS DISTINCT FROM old_reading THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Lesson 45 reading changed since audit baseline; refusing overwrite'; END IF;
  SELECT id INTO STRICT rid FROM public.readings WHERE lesson_id=lid ORDER BY sort_order,id LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE;
  UPDATE public.lesson_content_overrides SET content=jsonb_set(content,'{reading}',new_reading),version=version+1,updated_at=now() WHERE lesson_id=lid;
  UPDATE public.readings SET greek_text=(SELECT string_agg(p->>'greek',E'\n\n' ORDER BY n) FROM jsonb_array_elements(new_reading->'paragraphs') WITH ORDINALITY t(p,n)),translation=new_reading->>'translation',notes_markdown=new_reading->>'notesMarkdown',source_citation=new_reading->>'sourceCitation' WHERE id=rid;
  DELETE FROM public.reading_glosses WHERE lesson_id=lid AND reading_id=rid;
  FOR p IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(new_reading->'paragraphs') LOOP
    gi:=0;
    FOR g IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(p->'gloss') LOOP
      INSERT INTO public.reading_glosses (lesson_id,reading_id,greek,english,lemma,display_form,part_of_speech,morphology,source,sort_order)
      VALUES (lid,rid,g->>'greek',g->>'english',g->>'greek',g->>'greek','Reading gloss','{}'::jsonb,'lesson_reading_gloss',pi*1000+gi);
      gi:=gi+1;
    END LOOP;
    pi:=pi+1;
  END LOOP;
  UPDATE public.lesson_content_blocks b SET content=jsonb_set(b.content,'{value}',new_reading),updated_at=now()
  FROM public.lesson_segments s WHERE b.segment_id=s.id AND s.lesson_id=lid AND b.content->>'source'='lesson_publish' AND b.content->>'kind'='reading';
END
$audit45$;
COMMIT;
